gm-method/rendering (a raw-WebGL2 backdrop/planet renderer composited UNDER a three.js scene on the same canvas: 'objects draw over X even when behind/under X'). When a non-three GL pass renders into an offscreen FBO and blits COLOR-only to the canvas, three has NO depth to test against for that pass's geometry -> three objects always draw on top regardless of true depth. The pass usually stamps SOME depth via a gl_FragDepth writeback (so the main terrain occludes three), but a SECONDARY surface drawn color-only in that pass (water, transparent shells, a half-res effect layer) is MISSING from the shared depth -> only THAT surface fails to occlude. Diagnose at SOURCE by finding what the depth-writeback samples (it will be the primary depth texture, not the secondary surface's). FIX: re-draw the secondary surface DEPTH-ONLY (colorMask off, depthTest LESS, depthMask on) into the SAME depth attachment the writeback reads, before the writeback -- reusing the surface's own program/mesh (its VS already places it, its FS discard already clips it). Zero new textures, MSAA-safe (a shader depth pass, not a blitFramebuffer of depth which is GL_INVALID on single->MSAA). Gate it on the consumer's depth-share opt-in flag so non-sharing consumers pay nothing, and scope it to the exact path with the color-only gap (the straight-to-canvas path has native depth and was never broken). Witness with a per-frame counter incremented inside the new pass (proves it runs) + an A/B toggle of getError (proves it adds no GL error).